 ### Cross-View Pruning Mechanism
-A mechanism comprising a Cross-View Consistency Loss and adaptive pruning to reduce Gaussian redundancy. It selects adjacent view pairs with SSIM > 0.6, computes a consistency loss to penalize residual discrepancies, and prunes Gaussians based on depth/distance consistency conditions [S9].
+Reduces Gaussian redundancy and ensures geometric validity using a Cross-View Consistency Loss and adaptive pruning based on depth consistency [S9].
 
 ### Structure-View Co-learning
-A module combining structural and view-dependent gradients for robust joint optimization. The loss is $\mathcal{L} = \lambda (\lambda_V \mathcal{L}_V + \lambda_S \mathcal{L}_S)$, where $\mathcal{L}_V$ is view gradient loss and $\mathcal{L}_S$ is structure gradient loss [S9].
+Combines structural and view-dependent gradients for robust joint optimization of Gaussian positions and appearance [S9].
 
 ### Single-view Mini-batch Strategy
-A training data sampling strategy where data is organized per viewpoint, and a mini-batch consists of data from only a single viewpoint to improve efficiency [S10, unofficial].
+Organizes training data per viewpoint and samples mini-batches from only a single viewpoint to improve efficiency [S10, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training typically takes 15-60 minutes for standard datasets (e.g., Mip-NeRF 360) on modern GPUs, significantly faster than NeRFs which can take hours [S2][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of Gaussians. Default limit is 5,000,000 Gaussians [S3, unofficial]. Sinc splatting variants consume less memory than standard Gaussians [S7].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves real-time rendering speeds (100+ FPS) at 1080p on consumer hardware [S5, unofficial]. PSNR and SSIM are comparable to NeRFs [S2].
-*   **Scaling:** Scales to large scenes via hierarchical representations or optimized training loops (e.g., Msplat) [S4, S10, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Fast training, real-time rendering, high visual fidelity [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Can produce "floaters" or over-reconstruction in empty space; sensitive to initialization; requires careful densification tuning [S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When real-time rendering and fast training are priorities over absolute geometric accuracy or implicit surface extraction [S2].
-*   **When not to use:** When explicit mesh extraction is required without post-processing, or when dealing with highly dynamic scenes without temporal extensions [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training is significantly faster than NeRFs, often completing in under an hour for complex scenes [S5, unofficial]. The original implementation uses 30,000 iterations [S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of Gaussians. The default maximum is 5,000,000 Gaussians [S3, unofficial]. Sinc splatting variants consume less memory than standard Gaussians [S7].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves comparable image quality to NeRFs but with much faster training and real-time rendering speeds [S2][S5, unofficial].
+*   **Hardware:** Can run on CPU, GPU (CUDA), or Apple Silicon (MPS). CPU execution is ~100x slower than GPU [S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering, fast training, high-fidelity details [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Can produce "floaters" or over-reconstruction artifacts if not properly regularized [S2][S6, unofficial]. Sensitive to initialization and local minima [11914-mcmc-based-3d-gaussian-splatting.md].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S2]. It builds on the concept of radiance fields but replaces neural networks with explicit 3D Gaussians. The method initializes positions using sparse point clouds from SfM tools like COLMAP [S2][S6, unofficial]. It has since been extended to various domains including SLAM, dynamic scenes, and human avatars [S1].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. [9] (cited in [S1, S2]). It initializes positions using sparse point clouds from SfM tools like COLMAP [S2]. The method falls under the category of radiance fields but differs from NeRFs by using explicit Gaussian primitives [S5, unofficial]. It has been widely adopted for real-time rendering, SLAM, and dynamic scene reconstruction [S1].
 
